+### Creating a JSON Patch
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` returns a JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to` when applied with
+`applyPatch`, so a client can send only what changed between two versions of a document instead of the
+whole new version. Neither argument is mutated, and the patch shares no object or array with them.
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+createPatch(
+  { name: "Ann", roles: ["admin", "dev"], address: { city: "Paris", zip: "75001" } },
+  { name: "Ann", roles: ["owner", "admin", "dev"], address: { city: "Lyon", zip: "75001" } },
+);
+// [
+//   { op: "add", path: "/roles/0", value: "owner" },
+//   { op: "replace", path: "/address/city", value: "Lyon" },
+// ]
+```
+
+The patch holds only the differences, at the deepest location where the versions differ: members
+that are removed, added or changed, and array elements that are removed, inserted or changed. Arrays
+are aligned on their longest common subsequence, so inserting or removing one element does not
+rewrite the elements after it. A value whose type changes (an object that becomes an array, `1` that
+becomes `"1"`) is replaced; equal documents give an empty patch `[]`. The patch uses only `add`,
+`remove` and `replace`, and its array indexes are those of the array as the preceding operations
+leave it. When more than 1000 elements of two arrays would have to be removed or inserted, the arrays
+are compared by position instead, which keeps the cost bounded; the patch is still correct, only
+possibly longer.
+
+It throws a `JsonPatchError` (with `index` `undefined` and the underlying error as `cause`) when
+`from` or `to` is not a JSON value: only `null`, booleans, finite numbers, strings, arrays and plain
+objects are. See [ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-create-json-patch.md) for the design decisions.
+
