+# ADR 0004: Creating a JSON Patch
+
+- Version: 1
+- Date: 2026-10-22
+- Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document instead of the whole
+new version. RFC 6902 defines JSON Patch, which this library already applies
+([ADR 0002](0002-json-patch.md)), but not how to compute one: many patches turn a document into
+another, from a single `replace` of the root to one operation per changed scalar. Sending the whole
+document again when it differs in one small place is not acceptable.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **The patch describes each difference at the deepest location where the versions differ.** Objects
+   are compared member by member (removed members, then changed ones recursively, in the order of
+   `from`; then added members, in the order of `to`), and a value is replaced only when its type
+   differs or it is a different scalar. The patch thus grows with the size of the change, not of the
+   document.
+2. **Arrays are aligned on a longest common subsequence,** computed with Myers' O((N+M)·D) greedy
+   algorithm. Comparing arrays by position would turn the insertion of one element at the front into
+   a change of every element after it. Between two kept elements, the elements that differ are first
+   diffed pairwise in place (so a changed object inside a list gives a nested `replace`, not a removal
+   and an insertion of the whole object), then the surplus is removed or inserted. Myers' cost grows
+   with the number of differences D, so a small change to a long array stays cheap.
+3. **The alignment is bounded.** Its time and memory grow with D², so beyond 1000 removed and inserted
+   elements the arrays are compared by position instead. The patch is then still correct, only
+   possibly longer; arrays that differ that much are mostly rewritten anyway.
+4. **Only `add`, `remove` and `replace` are emitted.** Detecting moves and copies would make patches
+   shorter in rare cases at the cost of a much more complex search, and any patch made of these three
+   operations applies with `applyPatch` and any other RFC 6902 implementation. No `test` operations
+   are added: whether a client guards a patch is its decision.
+5. **Array indexes are those of the array being patched,** since RFC 6902 applies operations in
+   sequence; insertions use explicit indexes rather than `-`, so the patch reads the same everywhere.
+6. **Same contract as `applyPatch`.** Both inputs are validated as JSON values (ADR 0002 decision 3)
+   and deep-copied first, so neither is mutated and the values in the patch share nothing with them.
+   An invalid input throws a `JsonPatchError` with `index` `undefined`, as for an invalid document in
+   `applyPatch`, rather than a new error type. Members, including `__proto__`, are looked up as own
+   properties only (ADR 0002 decision 4).
+7. **Equality is that of the `test` operation** (RFC 6902 section 4.6), shared with JSON Patch in
+   `src/json-value.ts`, so a value `createPatch` treats as unchanged is one `test` would accept.
